======================================================================
Interactive usage
======================================================================

To start an interactive shell using the remote-ssh environment:

  bash --rcfile "@INSTALL_DIR@/shell/rc.sh"


======================================================================
SSH configuration
======================================================================

Add the following entry to your local SSH configuration
($HOME/.ssh/config):

  Host @HOSTNAME@
    HostName @IP_ADDRESS@
    User @WHO_AM_I@
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/<ssh-key>
    RequestTTY yes
    RemoteCommand bash --rcfile '@INSTALL_DIR@/shell/rc.sh' -i


Then connect normally:

  ssh @HOSTNAME@

This will start an interactive session with the remote-ssh shell
initialised automatically.


======================================================================
Optional Git setup
======================================================================

To enable the bundled Git defaults from `dots/git/config.base`, run:

  remote-ssh git setup

This creates `dots/git/user.local` from the example if needed and adds the
base config via `git config --global include.path ...`.

In remote-ssh shells, `dots/git/user.local` is also applied as a session Git
identity override. This affects Git commands in that SSH session without
writing to repository `.git/config` files.

It also creates `dots/ssh/config.local` from the example if needed and adds it
to `$HOME/.ssh/config` via `Include`. Edit `dots/git/user.local` and
`dots/ssh/config.local`, then point repository remotes at the alias:

  git remote set-url origin git@github.com-myuser:OWNER/REPO.git
